  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nWe begin the work week much like we ended it: seasonable (mid/upper \n70s for highs) and largely dry. The surface ridge stalled over the \nGreat Lakes drops further south and west by then, shunted in this \ndirection both by impulses in the longwave trough across the western \nCONUS/southern Canada and by the slight retrograde of the nor'easter \nongoing off the Northeast U.S. coastline. The longwave trough will \nalso serve to assist in the transport of tropical moisture from what \nremains of T.C. Odalys and T.C. Polo in the eastern Pacific (see NHC \nbulletins for more information). Precipitable water values are \nconsistently forecast to approach climatological maxima and couple \nwith deep southwest diffluence, two important large-scale factors \nneeded for an increased heavy rain threat. \n\nThe pattern currently modeled in a preponderance of ensemble and \ndeterministic guidance closely resembles those of predecessor rain \nevents (PREs) that allow for extremely efficient rainfall. \nResultantly, rain chances will be on the rise across northern \nMissouri and west-central Illinois by late Tuesday and become more \nwidespread by Wednesday. This general area, particularly northern \nand north-central Missouri, have the best chances locally for heavy \nrain and flooding (both flash flood and river) due to a combination \nof relatively moist soil (per NASA SPoRT analysis) and quality of \natmospheric moisture. Global-scale guidance is currently bearish on \nthe probabilities of high rainfall totals (for example, only a 30-\n40% chance of 2.00\" of rain at most per the NBM), partially due to \nits coarse spatial resolution and partially owing to uncertainty in \nwhere the heaviest rain falls. That said, model soundings leave \nlittle doubt in efficient warm rain processes that could push multi-\nday localized totals as high as 5.00 inches. Currently the \nexperimental WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook highlights northern \nMissouri in a 25-40% chance area (translating to the new \"Enhanced \nRisk\" in this product). \n\nFurther into the week, rain becomes more widespread (particularly \nlate Wednesday onward) as that remnant moisture lingers over the Mid-\nMississippi Valley. By this point, while rain chances are fairly \nhigh (60-80%) areawide, rainfall totals and the threat of flooding \nimpacts becomes more uncertain. Some guidance begins to suggest the \nquality of this moisture becomes more modified now that is several \ndays and over 1000 miles removed from its source region. If this \ncomes to fruition, while still more than sufficient for rain to \noccur, the efficiency of that rain would lessen and impacts would be \nmore limited. Still, some deterministic guidance and ensemble \nmembers point to that moisture staying very much in place with \nlittle modification. That scenario can't be discounted, as PREs have \na tendency to overperform and remain prolific longer than models \nsuggest. This threat will need to be monitored, however one factor \ndecidedly in our favor is the far drier conditions across the \nsouthern two-thirds of Missouri and southern Illinois. Regardless of \nwhich scenario prevails, even the more parched parts of the region \nsouth of I-70 can expect at leas 0.50 inches of rain (per the NBM). \nA quasi-zonal pattern looks poised to settle over the CONUS, opening \nour region to renewed rain chances through next weekend while \nkeeping temperatures in check as we start October.",
